 I'm Devin Sampson, and I'm here today with Kira Bush at her house in San Francisco.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:35.250
 I met Kira at a United Students for Fair Trade convergence about eight years ago, when most of us were talking about coffee and Kira was talking about rice.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:49.230
 And not just talking about rice, but she was there with rice farmers from Thailand talking about their efforts to challenge bilateral free trade agreements and also advocate for better market access for their rice.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:57.790
 She, at that time, was deeply involved in an organization called Engage, which is a kind of solidarity-oriented study abroad program.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:14.390
 She then worked at the White Earth Reservation in northern Minnesota following that rice theme where they farm a lot of wild rice, but working on community food sovereignty projects for about two years.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:28.340
 She got a master's in environmental science from Yale, where she did research on food justice curriculum, both in New Haven and in an indigenous community on the border of Panama and Colombia.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:37.530
 She now works at the Christensen Fund as the Program Officer in Agro Biodiversity, Food Sovereignty, and Resilient Biocultural Landscapes.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:41.720
 So we have a lot to talk about in the world of food.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.030
 So thanks for being here, and welcome to Delicious Revolution.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.720
 Thanks. Thanks for having me, Devin. It's so fun to do this.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:54.220
 I want to start, I want to start with Thailand.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:00.760
 How did you end up there, and what got you involved in agriculture?

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:19.450
 I had this immense privilege of going, first of all, to Indiana University, where I was part of a program that allowed me to study abroad for up to two semesters and every summer while I was there.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:29.070
 And having a bit of an adventurous spirit, I couldn't have been more excited to get away from Indiana and to whatever was out there waiting.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:38.310
 And I literally think that I played that game where you spin a globe around and landed on just about every country and went, yeah, I'm going to go there.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:48.130
 But went into a study abroad office to look at the programs that were available, and there was a really fascinating woman there.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:52.670
 I think she was thrilled to have me walk in because most people said they wanted to go to Australia or England.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.390
 And she said, you know, okay, do you speak any foreign languages?

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:57.870
 Okay, yeah, I speak foreign languages.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:04.650
 But she went through this list, and there was a program in Thailand run through CIEE.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:09.080
 Oh, I'll have to look up exactly what it stands for.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:09.480
 It's a study abroad program.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.300
 International Educational Exchange.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.000
 And it was meant to be different.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.900
 It was one of three programs they had at the time that was meant to be different.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.360
 It was all experiential.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.700
 The name of it, I will never forget.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.480
 It was the human perspective on development and the environment.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.340
 And at the time, I was making my own major in environmental and social justice.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.300
 I just couldn't imagine a better fit.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:37.790
 I thought I would study abroad for the whole year, but heard about that program and knew it was the right one.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:48.050
 So, changed my philosophy into this vision of realizing that Thailand had never been colonized.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.630
 I actually wanted to understand what that meant.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:06:07.840
 So, I spent a summer in West Africa and then a semester in Thailand and a semester in France, which had colonized West Africa, to try and understand this flow through colonialism, which may resurface later in all of these trade issues.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:25.630
 So, I arrived at this study abroad program, very, you know, still bright-eyed and bushy-tailed student in 19, 20, maybe.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:42.270
 I'd grown up in Indiana, where agriculture is not compelling, with all of the respect for all of the families who do this, which is decreasing and decreasing.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:54.010
 So, actually, it's a little bit of a sad picture because if you take a train ride through the Midwest, which I've done on numerous occasions, you see all of this farming infrastructure, silos, grain silos, storage, barns,

00:06:54.010 --> 00:07:17.130
 that are crumbling all along the railroads, the artery of this country at one point in time, at the same time that you are still surrounded by fields and fields of the most monotonous form of agriculture you can imagine, because it's built through a machine, you know, of corn and soy and long rows, which immediately presents this picture.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:23.350
 How can we have all of this infrastructure that's falling down and still all of these rows of corns?

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:27.550
 And that image sort of sticks at the heart of what's going on in global agriculture.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.540
 We're still growing huge amounts of food, but there's no people.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:39.920
 The people are gone from rural America, or the people are still there, but it's increasingly harder to make a living off of this form of farming.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:48.450
 So, we were asked in Thailand to facilitate a unit that we thought was the least compelling, being eager.

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:58.970
 I thought they were all compelling, and realized, you know, their gender rights, urban cities, water issues, dams, mining.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:09.080
 And then we got to agriculture, and I thought, yeah, I think I've effectively traveled literally halfway across the world from Indiana.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.940
 I don't need to study agriculture.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:13.520
 Let's pick that.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:16.400
 I'll facilitate that, and I'll pay attention in the other units.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:21.620
 So, that was my start down a path that I probably couldn't have imagined where it would take me.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:33.820
 So, someone asked you to choose the thing that was least compelling, and then you probably ended up, not too long after that, in an agricultural town.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:34.540
 Yeah.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.010
 So, all of these moments that change your mind.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:47.340
 I wish I knew the name of this gentleman, but not too long after that, we went to an agricultural town.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.120
 I had helped, you know, facilitate this unit.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.480
 We sat down in, it's a sala, is what it's called.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:09:03.760
 It's like an open-air, sort of like a rectangular gazebo in the middle of a field, you know, about 20 students, a few translators and sports staff.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:08.340
 And this gentleman welcomes us in, welcomes us into his home.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.500
 Before he feeds us, he wants to show us a few things, and he pulls out a photo album.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.450
 And I think, oh, man, this is going to be great.

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:24.230
 It's, like, his wedding photos, his grandchildren, I don't know, his water buffalo, like, what's going to be in there.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:28.360
 And he opens up the first page, and it's a picture of rice.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:36.440
 And, you know, in Indiana, we have, like, an expression about watching the corn grow, and it's like, oh, okay, maybe they watch the rice grow here.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:41.500
 And then the second page, it's the same variety of rice with the seed.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:46.890
 And he flips the page, and it's another picture of rice.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:50.610
 And he tells us how this rice is totally different.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:55.230
 You know, this first one is fragrant and tender and sweet.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:10:00.580
 And the second rice is fragrant and tender and just a little bit less sweet.

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:04.820
 And he goes through this whole photo album, and it's pictures of rice.

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:15.800
 And that is a moment I think I will never forget, the absolute care and love of this farmer for what he's doing.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:29.350
 And I think, as with many farmers everywhere in the world, those who love it, who are passionate about doing this kind of work, there's nothing they would rather be doing than growing food for themselves, for their families, for their communities.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:34.690
 And, you know, you see that in people keeping photo albums, and that's the first thing they want to share with visitors.

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:35.450
 Wow.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:45.540
 It reminds me of, I love, Virginia Nazaria has a book about cultural memory and biodiversity, and she opens it talking about a guy just like that, but in the Philippines.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:52.530
 And when I read that, she says, she calls them, like, she says, there's one in every village.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:56.850
 It's kind of thought about, it's kind of nutty, because they're really into their weird varieties.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.990
 But they keep that cultural tradition alive.

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:07.970
 And then she kind of suggests that there's probably going to be a time when we turn to those people for that kind of diversity.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:08.890
 Absolutely.

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:18.990
 As I started to learn more about these issues, and you mentioned biodiversity, rice, there's a lot of studies now that show how much biodiversity is shrinking, especially within food products.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:24.350
 We mainly use four major crops, something like that, with about 10 that are in regular circulation.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:25.670
 And this is in the globe.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:34.490
 You know, and rice was one of them where even 100 years ago, we had exponentially more varieties of rice than we have now.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:44.070
 And there were efforts in the 70s through the ERI, the International Rice Research Institute in the Philippines, to get samples of all of those different kinds of rice.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:59.220
 In fact, a lot of those efforts backfired in numerous ways, but one of which being there was a little bit of a, oh, if everything's being held in these places off-site, we don't need to do this, and undervaluing farmers like that and what they were doing for the biodiversity of the planet.

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:07.680
 There's another organization in Thailand that has systematically gone through and documented what varieties do people actually still have.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:27.440
 And so even though global studies will tell you that we're losing rice varieties and really all food varieties and crop wild relatives at these crazy rates, which we are, they were showing that a lot of these varieties are still out there, but with just one or two or 10 families or in circulation in a village.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:41.740
 And they were coming back, and they were coming back, and they made a rice encyclopedia, which is totally gorgeous, which is matching oral histories with pictures of rice and seeds, which is really useful in Thailand, but someday it'd be great to do something like that on a global scale.

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:47.530
 Totally. Well, it's a really similar feeling when you talk about corn with farmers in Yucatan.

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.070
 Yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:52.870
 And the low, the like, I had no idea that corn could taste all these different ways.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.250
 Yeah, it's true.

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:59.800
 Actually, I should say one thing, maybe make a transition for you.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:10.400
 Years later, I ended up going with the Thai farmer to White Earth in Minnesota to look at wild rice, and wild rice is amazing.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:11.720
 It isn't farmed.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:23.990
 So you have this absolute labor of love, people who, it is not an easy thing to do to transplant rice, to like bend down to the ground and separate these, you know,

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:28.030
 two-inch long stalks of rice from each other to make these even rows.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:35.370
 You can imagine why the idea of a combine and, you know, miles and acres and acres full of rows in the Midwest becomes more appealing.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:48.770
 But in Minnesota, the wild rice grows naturally in the riparian zone, so the shallow water is on the edge of the freshwater lakes, which is why it's only found in the Great Lakes region of the world.

00:13:48.770 --> 00:14:01.490
 And so going with a Thai farmer who's standing with, you know, Winona LaDuke, an indigenous activist, both for indigenous rights, but also for food globally, energy and a number of issues.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:04.930
 He's saying, okay, but where are your fields?

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:09.250
 And she was like, yeah, you're looking at it.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:11.560
 I love listening to her tell this story, actually.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:13.420
 And he's like, but where are the fields?

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:15.400
 Where do you grow your rice?

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:19.520
 And she's looking at him going, no, no, you're looking at it.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:22.560
 And I'm trying to, you know, translate this back and forth.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:26.820
 I think the guy looks at me at one point, his name is Uboniwa.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:28.000
 He's a wonderful activist.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.300
 His English is actually pretty good.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:33.040
 He's like, okay, Kira, you're just not translating this right.

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:34.480
 Let me try this in English.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:37.980
 Where is your rice?

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:42.420
 And she's like, yes, this is our rice.

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:54.760
 And so until I think we got out there in a canoe with the wooden knockers knocking the rice into the canoe, did he understand, oh, my God, they don't farm this.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:55.980
 It just grows.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:09.320
 And the reason you get it to regenerate, you knock with these wooden sticks about two feet long, about an arm length, into the water, well, into your canoe, but you lose, say, 30 to 40 percent into the water, which regenerates for the next year.

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:24.690
 And in fact, Red Lake, the reservation that is north of White Earth, has been known to shoot planes out of the sky who are trying to harvest wild rice by air because it doesn't do that same process.

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:30.750
 It basically wholesale harvests, which is incredibly dangerous to the regeneration cycle.

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:33.390
 Where you can harvest out of an airplane?

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:34.340
 Yeah.

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:36.580
 You know, I wish I knew the details of how this worked.

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:39.080
 I've been told that people tried this there.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:50.220
 And I think it has to do, yeah, with not airplane, but like a helicopter, like a hovercraft.

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:50.580
 Oh, wow.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:52.340
 I don't know.

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:53.900
 This is nuts to me.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:16:08.680
 But even if not that, people have tried to make the process by motorboat and by other things, which puts pollutants into the water, but also harvests a much higher percentage, which doesn't allow for regeneration to happen.

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:09.820
 And it's an iffy crop.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:16.710
 You know, if you get a big wind or a storm at the wrong time, all the rice falls down into water and you lose larger amounts.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:23.000
 But, you know, traditional wisdom over time, that evens out because the next year you have more growing.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:48.170
 Anyway, it was a really profound moment on both ends of that exchange to see, like, the gifts of the landscape and from the Anishinaabe worldview, the white earth or Anishinaabe people, that, you know, it's just taking care of the landscape is their task and the gift to the crater in order to provide food.

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:53.110
 Whereas in most of the world, farming communities, it's a huge amount of labor.

00:16:53.330 --> 00:17:09.850
 And I think actually in that story, in that little exchange is a little bit about what it means as a human to figure out how to feed yourself and how to sustain the environment that allows you to feed not just you, but a community and a network of people into the future.

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:25.060
 Yeah, I don't know. I've had it just makes me think about so many times the first times going to cornfields or home gardens in the Yucatan Peninsula and people being like, no, no, this is this is the garden because it just looks so much like a forest.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:33.210
 Yeah, I think that's right. I think we as we get more sophisticated, we understand that integrated landscapes are our future.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:40.600
 Sure. And I'm going to tell a quick story that maybe I shouldn't because, you know, the California drought is on everybody's minds.

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:46.340
 And for whatever reason, almonds seem to be like the victim crop, like they were bad in school.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:52.700
 And so this year, everybody is picking on almonds, like almonds are using too much water and almonds are doing this again.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:57.000
 Well, almonds are a tree crop. It takes like 20 years to get them there.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:05.170
 And in my opinion, it's it's not a problem with almonds like almonds didn't just suddenly act up.

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:16.510
 It's the way that we're planting them when you move to large, long row crops that are monotonous, that are that don't have biodiversity in it.

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:29.930
 You need to compensate for that by greater inputs, be them chemical like fertilizers or pesticides, because if almonds are your favorite snack as an insect and you see acres of them, it's a heyday.

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:32.130
 You know, and you have to compensate for that somehow.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:42.150
 You know, as you mentioned, a lot of times you see tree crops grown in forests, you know, actually scattered with other crops, just like companion planting.

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:47.130
 I think backyard gardeners will know if you're going to grow a tomato, maybe you want to put a marigold in with it.

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:50.390
 It's similar for everything we do as we scale up.

00:18:50.390 --> 00:19:02.110
 If you can have crops growing with forest cover, if you can have fruit and nuts, trees, you know, growing as cover to other crops, you have shared water usage.

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:03.830
 It's actually less water intensive.

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:12.370
 And so, you know, the problem with picking on almonds is it makes it seem like we could just substitute out almonds and put in walnuts because walnuts haven't been bad.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:31.590
 But walnuts are going to do the same thing in that system until we figure out how to create a system of food that is more integrated and is thinking about water systems and land and soil as all part of the same product and a longer term view of profits and feeding ourselves instead of just seasonal.

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:33.450
 That's a big ask.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:37.870
 Yeah.

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:40.920
 Yeah, well, let's talk about it then.

