 Yeah, Devin, that's a really great question.

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:18.210
 And I wish there were a few easy answers.

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:27.190
 I think when you start reimagining agriculture, there's a whole bunch of answers because you realize really quickly how much everything is interconnected.

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:39.690
 You know, I don't think I've mentioned it yet, but there's a real tie to the economy.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:41.890
 I suppose I mentioned it when thinking about Indiana.

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.210
 How do you have all these crops and no people?

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:51.530
 We have had, actually, I think it's now changed, fewer farmers than ever in the U.S.

00:20:51.530 --> 00:21:00.930
 at the same time that we had agricultural output, increasing a lot of that having to do with the NAFTA, fair trade agreements that happened when Bill Clinton was in office.

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:02.630
 It's the North American Free Trade Agreement.

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:10.220
 But you start to see a situation where, okay, so first of all, how has that changed?

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:26.190
 That's changed because I think in the last five to 10 years, I've heard, and the USDA keeps really great statistics on this, so we can check there, that farmers, numbers by number, not by percentage, numbers of farmers are increasing again.

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:42.420
 And so that's because where we had this trend where farms after the 1950s, incidentally, when we had lots of chemicals that we'd produced that we were then deciding that they could be useful by using them as fertilizers, which is true.

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:46.400
 And so that's what we're going to do, and we're going to be able to do, because we're going to do that, and we're going to do that, and we're going to do that in the United States.

