00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:54.800
 We're going to do that in the United States.

00:21:54.800 --> 00:21:55.800
 We're going to do that in the United States.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:22:01.670
 The economic flows, I guess, how do you make it viable?

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:06.290
 It starts with making it a job, making it something where somebody could stay where they are.

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:12.130
 And in the U.S., it's largely a job.

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:15.820
 In other parts of the world, it's access to land and land tenure.

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:22.940
 But I don't think that's a perfect division, because also in the U.S., you need access to land.

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:32.050
 And also in other parts of the country, or other parts of the world, you need cash income to prevent the alternative, which is migration.

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:38.270
 Very diverse farms take a lot of highly skilled labor.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:38.670
 Yeah.

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:40.730
 I think that's part of it.

00:22:41.410 --> 00:22:42.570
 Yeah, that's true.

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:48.770
 Highly skilled labor and labor period.

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:52.830
 You know, people have to be willing to, well, so two sides of that.

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:54.170
 One is you have to have kids.

00:22:54.290 --> 00:23:12.810
 You have to have young folks, people on the farm, or you have to fill those jobs with people who are willing to come and do sometimes not even highly skilled, but repetitive labor, you know, to kill each of those potato bugs by hand versus spraying the field.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:29.880
 I met this great farmer in Nebraska who was saying that there were three things that he needed, you know, that made, because he has maybe 200 acres of an organic farm, which is really large.

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:36.030
 And he was talking about the need to weed, the need for labor.

00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:43.270
 A lot of his neighbors didn't want to adopt this practice because they'd rather sit in their air-conditioned tractor and go through the field.

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:47.870
 It's easier, and they don't have kids to come help.

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:50.550
 But the other thing that they don't have are animals.

00:23:51.770 --> 00:24:04.620
 You know, if you can have chickens going through your rows doing some of the weeding for you, or you can have, you know, cows or some form of livestock, which happens in a lot of your more integrated, biodiverse farms.

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:11.080
 It's why they don't necessarily look as neat, and people look at pictures and farms in other parts of the world and may not understand it's a farm.

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:20.670
 If you can bring back in a lot of those elements of nature rather than a box, you see a lot of that happening.

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:25.610
 I think the biggest critique, people question whether that that can go to scale.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:34.970
 And I'm going to take us back to the beginning where you said at fair trade conferences, most people were talking about coffee and we were talking about rice.

00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:40.070
 And rice is amazing because you always have this surplus of it.

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:40.930
 You grow it.

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:42.880
 You don't need to grow that much.

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:52.620
 You can grow, you know, one hectare of rice and have more than enough for your family for a year and still sell it and still have it within an integrated system.

00:24:53.360 --> 00:25:00.850
 That doesn't mean in between each rice field is something, but there are rice fish growing systems.

00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:04.790
 It means around the borders of it, you have fruit trees or you have a little vegetable plot.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:12.210
 And on an amount of land that is tenable globally, you can still produce enough food to feed people.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:14.670
 So it's amazing to me.

00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:15.850
 It's, you know, a miracle.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:20.830
 But I think I've taken us a little bit off track.

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:24.670
 It's just a lot of mini stories within all of these stories.

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:44.520
 Well, let's talk about solidarity for a minute and working in solidarity with farmers movement and with other movements for connecting or reimagining what those economic supports could be for.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:44.920
 Yeah.

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:50.430
 A kind of agriculture that has a chance of feeding everybody well.

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:51.170
 Yeah.

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:53.930
 So you worked with this organization called Engage.

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:54.330
 Yeah.

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:56.890
 And that was it.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:26:00.930
 You went on a study abroad program, but then you started working on a study abroad program.

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:03.310
 Like, what was the promise that you saw in study abroad?

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:03.770
 Yeah.

00:26:03.830 --> 00:26:09.470
 So Engage, it stands for the Educational Network for Global and Grassroots Exchange.

00:26:09.470 --> 00:26:13.110
 And I think people went, they had very moving experiences.

00:26:13.350 --> 00:26:17.430
 You're able to, you know, I told the story about seeing Indiana in a new way.

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:24.090
 I think sometimes you have to go away from home to be able to see home more clearly.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:25.860
 And vice versa.

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:29.900
 Sometimes you travel somewhere else and you're able to see it with a different set of eyes.

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:37.830
 The power of exchange, you know, is always amazing and gives new innovations.

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:43.730
 And I'll tell an inverse story of one of the rice farmers I knew when I was in Thailand.

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:45.490
 Her name is Ganya Onshri.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:54.330
 She's also, incidentally, the first woman elected official for her whole province in southern Thailand.

00:26:54.590 --> 00:27:13.310
 She ran her cooperative, was the president of it for a while, rice farming cooperative, was a mother, woke up at about four every morning to move the water buffalo from one field to the next, make noodles for her kids before school, worked a full day on the farm, and then was incredibly tireless.

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:20.810
 She would stay up until often midnight visiting with students or anyone else who would come through to talk to them about what she was doing and why.

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:39.210
 She was part of a delegation that came to the United States to promote fair trade rice, help, I think, share stories of what it meant to community members to be adequately paid, compensated for the labor and the lack of inputs.

00:27:39.310 --> 00:27:40.530
 We talked a little bit about labor.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:43.590
 If you're not going to use chemicals, there's probably more labor.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:45.930
 The plus side of that is it creates jobs.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:50.770
 The downside of that is if you don't have people, it's very difficult to do that.

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:55.650
 And not everybody wants to do that kind of labor all the time, so it's always balancing an equation.

00:27:57.230 --> 00:28:10.500
 So she came to the U.S., spoke around the country, saw a lot of university students, learned about fair trade in a more comprehensive way, and she was really struck by going to Maine.

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:18.480
 Maine is one of those places we talked about earlier where you have an increase of farmers, small-scale, small farms.

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:29.120
 And a lot of them are young, college-educated, liberal arts college students who decided that they wanted more or less a reincarnation of the Back to the Landers.

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:36.860
 What happens next is, I think, a different story, but she went and saw that and had never seen anything like that.

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:43.910
 In Thailand, farmers don't have college educations, and college-educated students don't come back to farm.

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:51.090
 So this paradigm was new for her and was so exciting.

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:55.530
 And she thought about, what if the kids in our village could do this?

00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:59.410
 And she said, you know, they don't all have to come back and be farmers.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:06.110
 I want them to be whatever they want to be, but I don't want them to lose the knowledge that goes with being a farmer.

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:08.830
 And that doesn't just mean knowing how to grow rice.

00:29:09.110 --> 00:29:14.270
 It means knowing which mushrooms you can gather in the forest at which time of year.

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:24.810
 You know, it means how to bake that into the, like, sticky rice treats that you give to the monks as part of your ceremony of honoring it, or which plants you need to do the ceremonies.

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:34.850
 All of these different community rituals that go into being a good neighbor, helping plant for elders who can no longer do it.

00:29:35.150 --> 00:29:39.530
 So she came back and started a camp for kids.

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:46.710
 So it was a free after-school program, and during summer breaks for kids who were 8 to 12.

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:51.110
 Incidentally, they named themselves the Kids Love Nature Group.

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:54.230
 But to learn how to do all of these things.

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:57.790
 So, okay, power of exchange on so many levels.

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:02.470
 This is an idea that she saw elsewhere that she was able to come back and implement.

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:09.450
 I was able to visit with her again for the first time in about 10 years last year.

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:12.460
 It was incredible to see what's happened there.

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:14.500
 Her daughter is now in college.

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:19.720
 And her daughter is one of the first people from their village to go to college.

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:22.420
 And she is studying animal agriculture.

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:29.500
 She's out of school, and she's learning about how to take care of pigs, which was the next project that happened.

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:33.380
 With the extra funds you get from Fairtrade, they brought pigs into the community.

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:38.640
 So it's just an amazing, amazing circle.

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:41.530
 That's in one person's life.

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:48.140
 I think exchange and solidarity works community to community level and country to country level.

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:54.920
 There's just so many stories that I think I could share on that.

00:30:54.980 --> 00:31:08.250
 But I think it really probably boils down to interconnection as you start to understand that, you know, what's happening on a farm in Thailand is affected by the U.S. farm bill.

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:14.010
 And our farm policy is affected to what we do with all of that corn that we're growing in a mechanized fashion.

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:16.070
 And then we need to set a place to sell.

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:29.710
 You realize, you know, I don't know, more and more, I suppose, you start to see, without oversimplifying it, that little sentiment about, you know, when a butterfly flaps its wings.

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:35.690
 Anyway, you can see these outward ripple effects of any action.

00:31:37.150 --> 00:31:45.830
 And exchange helps you both to see your own place in that and also how true it is that these actions can't be totally teased apart from one another.

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:54.350
 Yeah, it seems to me like going and seeing a different part of the world is a really good idea.

00:31:54.490 --> 00:32:03.730
 But also there's like a way of, there's a way of going and seeing a different part of the world that helps to make that, see things as connected.

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:06.200
 Yes. Yeah, that's true.

00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:16.990
 I think it's, it's, it's all too easy to go someplace and just see snapshots or just see what you want to see and reinforce your own beliefs.

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:23.110
 I ask just because it seems like you were finding ways to teach that way of seeing.

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:24.950
 Right. Yeah, with engage.

00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:31.400
 So I'd like to think that we found ways.

00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:40.270
 I think it takes, some of these things take a long time for, for, for anyone, you know, to, you see something.

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:42.330
 Something new, some things strike you immediately.

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:46.590
 Many things take years to really fully settle and see.

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:55.240
 And, you know, it took me years to understand how powerful I think it was to look at one man's photo album of, of rice.

00:32:55.720 --> 00:32:58.400
 Instead of to think, okay, when are we going to eat?

00:32:58.500 --> 00:32:59.020
 I'm hungry.

00:32:59.320 --> 00:32:59.720
 Right.

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:00.960
 Which is what you think.

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:03.540
 Or what some people think.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:05.960
 And, and so how do you teach that way?

00:33:06.040 --> 00:33:20.420
 I think it does help to have hosts who are endlessly patient and willing to help translate through cultures and through experiences and to have ways that you can then translate that back into action in your own life.

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:23.940
 So I think that's what engage tried to do.

00:33:24.260 --> 00:33:30.480
 You have, you know, what here in Silicon Valley, people will now call a disruptive experience of studying abroad.

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:35.430
 But what's the purpose of that disruption if you aren't going to translate it into something?

00:33:36.010 --> 00:33:42.760
 And whether that is a new understanding in your own community or keeping ties with, with that community.

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:44.720
 And I think that happens in two ways.

00:33:44.820 --> 00:33:47.370
 Sometimes people become allies.

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:50.050
 They're allies to those communities for life.

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:57.700
 Actually, in a couple of weeks, there's, there's been a proposed mine in one of these same communities in Thailand.

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:15.820
 And those folks, because of the actions of this network of returned study abroad individuals, which is now, you know, hundreds and hundreds strong over a decade, are going to travel to Oaxaca to exchange with folks there about what they've done in the exact same situation.

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:23.610
 Well, a similar situation of, of trying to prevent a mine from coming in to their community.

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:34.160
 So there's, and there's a lot more to be said about things like that, but there's that kind of solidarity action or that kind of ally directly to those communities.

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:41.540
 But there's also, I think, a kind of ally who people see those actors, they take those lessons and they do that in their own life.

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:52.840
 People decide that they're going to be farmers in Maine because they saw what it meant to, to work in a system where agriculture means you walk outside your front door and you pick a pomegranate here, you know,

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:56.240
 and you get like an eggplant, fresh eggplant from over there.

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:59.320
 It's like living in a grocery wonderland.

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:03.720
 You know, the farmer's market is at your fingertips every time you walk in and out of your house.

00:35:04.760 --> 00:35:08.720
 People get inspired in that way, or, you know, that's an agricultural example.

00:35:08.720 --> 00:35:21.860
 But I think, I think this, the network of Engage was trying to find ways to help people translate those lessons into their life and share calls for help when, when they were needed, when environmentalists were under threat.

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:41.620
 Um, which sadly happened, um, and happens, um, or when, you know, when there's other sorts of, um, more basic life things that happen, uh, basic is the wrong word, but, um, you know, somebody gets cancer and, and, um, their family needs help.

00:35:41.620 --> 00:35:50.330
 Um, just humanitarian action of, of the, the kind that you make when you connected with an individual, no matter where, or who, or how.

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:56.250
 You, you've done a lot of community organizing.

00:35:56.710 --> 00:36:05.300
 You've done a lot of activism in it, and I imagine that you're still doing those things, but you're also now working for, uh, for a funding organization.

00:36:05.540 --> 00:36:15.030
 Um, what's, you're working for at the Christensen Fund, um, which is one of the most progressive and dedicated supporters of agri-ecology and small-scale agriculture.

00:36:15.230 --> 00:36:15.510
 Yeah.

00:36:15.590 --> 00:36:21.310
 But, but what's that transition like, or what's, what's the tension like for you now working with a funding organization?

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:35.770
 Uh, the transition was, was, um, seamless and, and not seamless all at the same time, um, all of the same issues, um, but from a different vantage point.

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:40.790
 And there's the personal transition, and then there's the, the transition in thinking.

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:51.950
 Um, when, so I spent two years working on community food programs at White Earth with an organization called the White Earth Land Recovery Project.

00:36:51.950 --> 00:37:10.470
 Um, and that meant everything from figuring out how to deliver, um, diabetic-friendly foods to, to elders with diabetes, to working at a cafe and sourcing local food, um, and traditional foods, and setting up a farm to school program that did the same thing, took out high fructose corn syrup and replaced it with traditional foods.

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:17.990
 Um, when you work on that scale of problem, it's, here is my problem, and how do I fix it?

00:37:17.990 --> 00:37:21.650
 And here is the next problem, and oh my God, here's the next and the next and the next and the next.

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:23.190
 And it's like firefighting, right?

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:33.390
 There's never, you never stop the pipeline of need, um, or it's, it's hard, it's hard to stop.

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:42.450
 There's always suffering happening, um, but there's also, you know, there's just communities always, there's always ways to improve on that level.

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:50.900
 Um, when you start working for a foundation, I think a lot of times you can think about that same problem and think, why?

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:59.000
 Um, as you work for a community organization, you're acutely aware of why, where is the economic system stacked against you?

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:09.640
 You know, where the fact that it's a rural community where jobs are hard means there isn't a lot of capital coming in to start to address and make changes on a community level that you would see in a wealthier community.

00:38:10.180 --> 00:38:16.360
 Um, when you work at a foundation, you have the luxury of doing something about the why, right?

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:21.340
 So you can move upstream and think about what is a five-year solution to this problem?

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:23.320
 What is a 20-year solution to the problem?

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:26.060
 What is a hundred-year solution to this problem?

00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:36.880
 In my vision, what could communities, and I say my, but I mean are much more collectively, you know, and are being a set of stakeholders working on these issues.

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:52.410
 What does it look like a hundred years from now to have sustainable food systems, to have, you know, we use this lofty language, like resilient biocultural landscapes, because it's, I think, to provoke that question, what does food sovereignty mean?

00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:57.850
 What does it mean to have a resilient landscape that can handle the shocks that we know are going to come?

00:38:58.340 --> 00:39:04.360
 And it's not that we can prevent them, but we can think about how we handle them more effectively on a community level.

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:07.930
 So, um, we get to think about that now.

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:11.910
 I think as a foundation, we think about land tenure.

00:39:12.470 --> 00:39:28.270
 Um, how can folks continue to not only farm or forage or hunt or fish on the lands and waters in their community, but have access to stewardship, um, to, to continuing to keep those lands and waters healthy into the future.

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:58.690
 Uh, we think about how they can connect to each other, to link, to, to share best practices, um, which is another part of exchange that I think, you know, I haven't touched on, but there's lots of, um, campesino to campesino efforts, especially in Latin America, but in other parts of the world with, by different names, um, farmer field schools, agroecology field schools, where folks who are doing innovations, um, at a scale that's manageable and transferable can transfer that to other, other folks.

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:14.150
 You know, here's an example from the Hopi, um, who have been growing corn here in the Americas for as long as there's been habitation in the Americas, um, who've been doing it without water in a dry land farming system.

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:27.980
 As we get increasing drought here in the U.S., um, with changing climate and other factors, we're going to have to know how we grow corn and everything else that we grow with less water.

00:40:27.980 --> 00:40:46.390
 So I think we're increasingly looking to the wisdom of folks who've stayed on the lands, you know, to the farmer in the Philippines, to the farmer, um, to the Hopi farmer to say, okay, not because we're going to imagine turning things back, but because we're going to imagine going into the future, you know, what is the wisdom of how we do this and how do we deal with our circumstances now?

00:40:47.420 --> 00:41:02.190
 So all of these things are things that, that you get the, um, privilege of working on at a foundation because you can move from one community to a set of global communities.

00:41:02.190 --> 00:41:18.730
 Um, I see, um, I see the types of things that people are working on, uh, globally now, and you start to be able to see patterns that, um, you know, other people don't have access to see, you know, you're too, you're busy fighting your own very valid fire.

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:26.090
 It's hard to know, not only you recognize there are fires across the world, but you don't always get to see how people are starting to solve them.

00:41:26.090 --> 00:41:43.070
 Um, so as we, you know, get to see these patterns, I think we are able to, um, in a best case scenario, share them with other people who can use that information, um, help create networks that, that will last as well as to think about strategic levers of where policy change is needed.

00:41:43.610 --> 00:41:56.630
 Um, you know, where institutions are needed to help knowledge live, um, um, and be practiced, um, other sorts of answers like that.

00:41:56.630 --> 00:42:26.060
 Yeah, it seems to me like when we think about on the global scale agriculture, um, there's, we're, we're at a really interesting time right now because there's one narrative that feel, first of all, there's more attention to this idea of like, how, how should agriculture work and how are we going to eat in that long-term timeframe than, than I ever could have imagined just 10 years ago.

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:47.850
 Right. But there's one narrative that's really strong that says, we're going to do that the same, that, that long rows of corn and soybeans way. And there's this other narrative that just right now feels like it's like, I hear, I heard the word agroecology come out of the mouth of the new minister of agriculture in Mexico, which I never could have imagined.

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:59.060
 But there's this other vision for the next hundred years or the next, the long-term way that this totally different. Do you feel like it's a, it's a strange time in the energy of?

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:16.990
 I think it's a, I think it is a time where we're, I think, I think you're right. We're seeing, um, we're seeing new acknowledgements. Um, you know, the, the sort of tried and true narrative is that we don't have enough food and we're, we're not going to feed people and that we have to do that by increasing yields.

00:43:16.990 --> 00:43:37.560
 And we have to increase yields by, you know, adding more chemicals and using this, this, this model of agriculture, um, that the green revolution really championed. And I think that we're seeing now, um, other explanations. You know, we talked about how there's a little bit of a balancing of the scales, labor versus inputs always, you know, no matter how you define that.

00:43:37.560 --> 00:44:07.540
 And I think people are now thinking about, well, what does the other side look like? You've got lots of countries like Japan where there are fewer and fewer children, period. And so aging populations still need to feed itself. Islands, you know, how do you import? Where are you growing agriculture? Where does this come from? Um, it's not just the minister of agriculture who, um, or the minister in Mexico. Um, the United Kingdom has, has been talking about agroecology. Um, even in its parliament, um, France has introduced new rules about agroecology and agroecology.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:38.360
 Agroforestry. Even I think people are, when they think about how they're going to feed themselves. Um, you know, the U S is, is a country that has this immense luxury of land, more arable land than just about any other country and more diversity of crops. And so we can, um, it can, it can mask, it can mask failures, um, maybe for a little bit longer, but also we have lots of options. A lot of places don't have lots of options and land.

00:44:38.360 --> 00:45:08.430
 And isn't one of them, you know, so how are you going to be creative with the resources you have is a more preliminary question. And you've seen time and time again, that agroecology is one of those ways that it is producing enough that it is profitable. Um, and I think there are huge efforts now. Um, there have been in, in several countries, several African countries efforts, um, of a repo, um, which is seed harmonization acts, um, which try and

00:45:08.430 --> 00:45:30.860
 keep boiling it down a little bit too simplistically. So, but basically keeping seeds away from individuals and standardizing the kinds of seeds you can use. And I think there's a huge, massive protest against things like that for agroecological methods that say, no, we're more likely to get better innovation, to find better answers to how we need to get, you know, seeds that adopt to new conditions.

00:45:30.860 --> 00:45:40.860
 If many, many, many people are doing this. And I'm going to use an analogy of crowdsourcing, you know, Wikipedia is brilliant.

00:45:40.860 --> 00:46:11.210
 Some dude in the Netherlands or where is he? Denmark is brilliant. But Wikipedia works because there are millions of brains who can add lots of little pieces of knowledge. No, one of those individuals know anything that's on Wikipedia beyond what they've added maybe, but that collection of knowledge. And if we could do a similar thing with seeds, you know, if you could have globally farmers, millions of farmers who keep the photo albums out there.

00:46:11.230 --> 00:46:24.390
 Tinkering, remembering what they're tinkering, you may have a chance of getting much more effective seeds, you know, and therefore growth and yields appropriate for the region that they're in than, you know, having a few people tinker with genomes in factories.

00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:42.070
 And that doesn't mean you have to stop all forms of a standard moderate model of agriculture. It means you have to think more broadly about how you're going to get lots of different solutions to problems because we know the circumstances continue to change.

00:46:42.070 --> 00:47:03.060
 And, you know, personally, I would always vote for having more brains and more hands working on any sort of solution, especially when we think about the fact that in the Midwest, as much as in, say, Uganda, you know, there's there are just communities that aren't being reached by one model.

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:16.990
 And, you know, maybe this makes me like the opposite of progressive. I think if we sit around and wait for a government to, like, provide answers to all of these solutions, that's going to take a long time.

00:47:16.990 --> 00:47:27.350
 And so I just think I really believe in the power of communities and individuals to tackle some of these problems until they get some sort of other buy in and support.

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:39.990
 Awesome. So that support. Oh, man, this is this has been great. This is we're at 45 minutes, but I do want to ask one more question.

00:47:40.070 --> 00:47:57.430
 So, like, how does that support work? So if we're talking about without without going too far into it, but you've been involved for a couple of years now in working on how to make that support work in a really detailed way.

00:47:57.600 --> 00:48:05.940
 Yeah. Boy, I wish I had all the answers. But, you know, we're trying. We're experimenting on a lot of them.

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:10.480
 I feel really lucky in my role now to get to work with a lot of other foundations.

00:48:10.480 --> 00:48:15.240
 So each one of us slices and analyzes these problems a little differently.

00:48:15.440 --> 00:48:29.000
 I've we've talked a lot about the production and of food sovereignty, but there's an equal end of thinking about community health and this integration of individual health and well-being with landscape health and well-being and community health and well-being.

00:48:29.500 --> 00:48:40.910
 Really, all of which are needed for sustainability and agroecology and some I think, you know, maybe the narrative is more common in the US about healthy eating and healthy foods and what it means.

00:48:40.910 --> 00:48:54.130
 But and those connections. We've we've we've we've been a part of several collaboratives with other donors in an effort to help get resources to folks.

00:48:54.130 --> 00:48:59.870
 So if I say, you know, it's all well and good for us to sit in San Francisco and say, don't wait for the government.

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:02.570
 But, you know, people still need food on the table now. Right.

00:49:02.630 --> 00:49:08.910
 And they still need resources that make that possible in the absence of this beautiful vision of a green jobs economy.

00:49:09.950 --> 00:49:17.210
 And I think that's where private foundations and investors can often step in and help bridge that gap in an ideal world.

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:21.250
 Not every gap, not every situation, but certainly we can try.

00:49:21.370 --> 00:49:24.330
 And this is an area in which I think folks are interested in trying.

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:33.640
 You know, private foundations did a lot for the, in quotes, feed the world movements that you saw in the 60s, 70s, 80s and beyond.

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:37.360
 Philanthropy has always been interested, I think, in feeding children.

00:49:37.360 --> 00:49:39.980
 And now it's a different tack on how you can do that.

00:49:40.550 --> 00:49:49.930
 So we're part of a multi-donor fund called the Agroecology Fund that's looking at collaborations across scale and research, advocacy and policy.

00:49:50.630 --> 00:50:04.270
 Another one called the Food and Farming Communications Fund, trying to look for innovative communications that are addressing not just communication techniques, but but often, you know, ways that we can communicate these messages out more broadly

00:50:04.270 --> 00:50:08.240
 to different communities and engage them.

00:50:10.080 --> 00:50:15.250
 I think funding small and large hits a lot of different issues.

00:50:15.250 --> 00:50:34.150
 There's 30 or 40 foundations now working together in a group called the Global Alliance for the Future of Food that's focusing on, again, lots of words, the agroecological transitions, health and well-being, and externalities and true cost accounting in the food system.

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:54.340
 Again, those are not the only problems, but those are some of the key areas where I think if we can get a lot of smart brains together, thinking about how they can align their work, we can we can see motion, we can see a food system that brings in some of the negative externalities that aren't included now.

00:50:54.340 --> 00:51:04.520
 And some of the positive externalities of different forms of agriculture, we can think about health and well-being, not just crop yield, you know, and we can think about changing these practices.

00:51:04.520 --> 00:51:32.960
 So I guess that's the goal, you know, when I talk about 10, 20-year solutions, a lot of it is thinking about these strategies that could move us towards a world where, you know, the farmer who wants their kids to be able to stay at home with a college education and do whatever they want is possible, you know, who can feed, who are fed and happy themselves, but also who are able to feed others.

00:51:32.960 --> 00:51:35.140
 Yes, Kira, thanks so much.

00:51:35.220 --> 00:51:36.200
 This has been great.

00:51:37.650 --> 00:51:47.380
 Just one last question is like, for people who want to follow along with the work that you're doing, the work that the Christiansen Fund is doing, the work that Engage is doing, where should people go to follow along?

00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:49.020
 Oh, good question.

00:51:50.740 --> 00:51:51.940
 Engagegrassroots.org.

